Cities are captured: a combat unit that enters an enemy city takes it (CityInteractions.CaptureCity) instead of destroying it, as in Civilization III. The city loses a citizen, and one of size 1 is destroyed instead. Its palace and small wonders, which belong to the civ that built them, are lost (the old owner is left without a capital, as when its capital is destroyed); great wonders and other buildings stay. The production and food boxes empty and the new owner's ChooseProducible picks what to build. The old owner's units still in the city are lost, as when a city is destroyed. The citizens keep their nationality and are re-assigned to the new owner's tiles, and the borders are recomputed from the new owner's culture in the city (City.perPlayerCulture): the tiles it owned under its old owner, and the fringe Laws VII and VIII added, are cleared first. MsgCityCaptured tells a UI. A taken city still counts as a city lost in the war (PlayerRelationship.RecordLoss). Barbarians still only take gold. Taking a city draws nothing from GameData.rng beyond what ChooseProducible and tile assignment draw when a city is built.

